<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfonso V of León</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">994–1028</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfonso V was king of León from 999 to 1028, coming to the throne as a child after the death of his father Bermudo II. He rebuilt the city of León after the raids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a> and in 1017 granted it a fuero, one of the earliest Spanish codes of municipal law. He was killed by an arrow while besieging Viseu in Muslim-held Portugal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-v-of-leon.jpg" alt="Alfonso V of León" width="320" height="550" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Acoma · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfonso_V_of_Le%C3%B3n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Alfonso V</b>'s fuero of León, issued at a royal council in 1017 or 1020, set out rules for the city and the kingdom and became a model for later charters.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
